notes:
  - "The App-level grace→warn assertion mocks Date.now rather than using fake timers: the screen re-judges traffic on a real one-second setInterval, and testing-library's waitFor cannot drive vitest's fake clock through jsdom's animation-frame callbacks (CodeMirror's pre-existing getClientRects error in this file is the same jsdom limit). The mock moves the clock the tick reads, and waitFor's 3 s ceiling covers the tick. The unit-level recency tests pass an explicit `now`, which is the honest shape for a pure resolver (DEC-20)."
  - "jsdom has no Tauri, so in App.test both probes resolve `unknown` (the mocked invoke returns undefined and the component's default applies). The rewritten connection test therefore asserts the proxy step reads 'can't check automatically' — its probe's verdict — rather than 'not set'; what it proves is that the tunnel did not move it, which is the claim."
  - "Two assertion mistakes of mine this round, both caught by the suite rather than by me: getByText on 'checked at HH:MM:SS' failed because TimestampText splits the text across two elements (fixed by asserting the row's textContent), and I first asserted the jsdom proxy step reads 'not set on this machine yet' when its probe is unknown there. Neither changed the product."
  - "Not tested and stated as such: the real focus event from macOS re-probing the machine (the listener is asserted with a synthetic window focus event), the visual rhythm of the ↓ inside a step line at 24 px, and the 30 s / 3 s constants as product choices — they are asserted as the boundary the code honours, not as the right numbers; DEC-33 records why they were chosen."
